# Overview
#
# `please` softens an instruction but does not add content to it
# (`Please restart the server`), so the reader gains nothing from the
# word. The technical-writing skill bans it. The fix deletes the word
# and gives the instruction. The rule sits at Recommended, because a
# README invites contributions with `please`, the way the Recommended
# ProseChatbot keeps `feel free to`.
#
# Expression
#
# A space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so the guard sits glued to
# `please` and reads the token right after it. The guard skips a space
# and `note`, and it skips a period, a semicolon, a colon, and a comma.
#
# False Positives
#
# Before one of those punctuation marks, `please` means `wish` (`the
# threads set the mask as they please`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Required ProseFiller reports `please note`, so the guard skips it.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 114 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 17 of 20 sampled
# hits softened an instruction. The three misfires invited a
# contribution (`please file an issue`).
expr main please![(( )note), ., ;, :, (,)]
let message "`please`. Delete it and give the instruction."
let description "Flags `please`, which softens an instruction without changing it."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Open an issue for any bug you find."
allows "The pleased reviewer approved the change."
allows "The threads set the mask as they please."
